<TopicPage slug="subjects/data-science/advanced/statistics-for-data-science">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Data science runs on statistics whether or not it admits it. Machine learning is regression
    and classification at scale; A/B testing is hypothesis testing with a dashboard; anomaly
    detection is distribution thinking. This lesson maps the statistics you already have (the
    statistics ladder) onto the questions data science asks daily.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four statistical habits of effective data scientists" widget="equationStep" config={statCfg} />

  <h2>The mappings that matter</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Sampling distributions → dashboards:</strong> any metric computed on users is a sample statistic. A "3.2% conversion" on 400 users has a ±1.7-point interval — dashboards should show it (the sampling lesson's formula).</li>
    <li><strong>Regression → attribution questions:</strong> "which channel drives signups?" is a regression with confounders — and the causal-inference lesson is the honest manual when randomisation isn't available.</li>
    <li><strong>Distributions → anomaly detection:</strong> most "AI anomaly detection" starts as "how many standard deviations from normal?" — knowing your distribution's shape comes first.</li>
    <li><strong>Bayesian updating → metrics in flux:</strong> early A/B results are priors being updated; the Bayesian lesson explains why small early "wins" reverse.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The communication standard</h2>
  <p>
    Numbers leave your notebook and enter decisions. The professional format: point estimate,
    interval, sample size, and what would change the conclusion. "Signups up 12% (95% CI
    4–19%, n = 12,400)" is a sentence a decision-maker can act on — "signups up!" is not.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Wasserman, All of Statistics; Efron & Hastie, Computer Age Statistical Inference; the statistics ladder of this atlas.</p>
</TopicPage>
